import
	../../html

intro-types html[]()
	- section "Type annotations",

		- p quote
			Types come after the name of the thing they are the type of.
			{br}
			Function return types and parameter types are required.
			Local variable types are optional.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log foo 1

			foo string(n nat)
				s string = "\{n\}"
				s

		- p quote
			Above, {fn-main} is {void}, {fn-foo} is {string},
			{variable "n"} is {nat}, and {variable "s"} is {string}.

	- section "Anonymous type annotations",
		- p quote
			An anonymous type annotation that attaches to an expression instead of to a name.
			{br}
			{variable "expr"}{colon-colon}{type "type"} specifies the type of an expression.
			{br}
			In a function call like {fn-to}{colon-colon}{int}{sp}{number "1.5"},
			the type is the return type of the function.
			{br}
			You can also write {colon-colon}{type " type"} at the start of an indented block to type the block.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				n = 1::nat
				m = to::int 1.5
				log
					# This applies to the expression below it, not to 'log'
					:: string
					"\{n\} \{m\}"

		- aside-small "If {colon-colon} were an operator it would be the most tightly binding one."

	- section "Expected types",
		- p quote
			When Keen type-checks an expression, it may have an {em "expected type"}.
		- bullets
			- "A function body's expected type is the function's return type."
			- quote
				A function argument's expected type is the function's declared argument type.
				{br}
				(Overloading complicates this; this will be explained in {intro-link overloading}.)
			- "A local's expected type is the declared type (if any)."
			- "Any expression with an anonymous type annotation has that as an expected type."
			- quote
				Certain expressions have an expected type;
				for example, in a sequence expression, any subexpression but the last must be {void}.
		- p quote
			The below example uses {parens} which can take on many types.
			This will be fully explained in {intro-link new_}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				() # is void
				# '()' is a bool
				_ bool = ()
				# '()' is a nat
				_ = foo ()

			foo string(_ nat)
				() # is a string

		- p quote
			Many expressions do not need an expected type.
			{br}
			For example, the call to {fn-foo} does not need an expected type,
			since there is only one {fn-foo} and it returns a {string}.

	- section "Literal expressions",
		- p quote
			Literal expressions have favorite types that they will use automatically if there is no expected type.
		- p quote
			A number literal prefers to be a {nat}, but a negative number will be an {int}
			and a number with a decimal point (even if {number ".0"}) will be a {float}.
		- p "A string literal prefers to be a {string}."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				n = 1 # nat
				i = -1 # int
				f = 1.1 # float
				s = "hello" # string
				log n, i, f, s

		- p quote
			If there is an expected type, a literal expression will use that instead of its default type.
			{br}
			(More on expected types in {intro-link overloading}.)

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				f float = 1
				s symbol = "hello"
				j json = 4
				log f, s, j

		- p "The possible number literal types are:"
		- bullets
			- "{nat8}, {nat16}, {nat32}, {nat64} ({nat} is an alias for {nat64})"
			- "{int8}, {int16}, {int32}, {int64} ({int} is an alias for {int64})"
			- "{float32}, {float64} ({float} is an alias for {type "float64"})"
			- "{json}"
		- p "The possible string literal types are:"
		- bullets
			- "{string}"
			- "{symbol}"
			- "{char8}, {type "char8[]"}"
			- "{char32}, {type "char32[]"}"
			- "{json}"
		- p quote
			For {char8} or {char32}, the string literal must contain a single character.
			{br}
			String literals can even be a user-defined type; see {intro-link strings}.

	- section "Implicit conversion of numbers",
		- p quote
			Number types implicitly convert to larger number types of the same kind.
			{br}
			Keen will not implicitly convert between {nat}s, {int}s, and {float}s, just within each category.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				n8 nat8 = 1
				n nat = n8
				i8 int8 = n.to
				i int = i8
				f32 float32 = i.to
				f float = f32
				log f

		- aside-small "When you do need a conversion function, it is usually named {fn-to}."
